          cite:"FCA §115; NY Courts Family Court case basics",
          url:"https://www.nycourts.gov/help/family-issues-divorce/family-court-case-basics",
          intro:{"idea":"Family Court hears a specific list of family matters. It alone hears abuse and neglect, support, paternity, PINS and juvenile delinquency cases, and the cases to permanently end parental rights. It shares family offense cases with the criminal courts. In a divorce, it decides support, custody and similar issues only when the supreme court refers them.","example":"A clerk gets three papers: a support petition, a family offense petition, and a request for a divorce. The support case belongs to Family Court alone. The family offense case can go to Family Court or to criminal court. The divorce goes to supreme court; Family Court only gets issues like custody from a divorce when the supreme court refers them.","source":"FCA 115"},
          source:{"cite":"FCA 115(a), (b), (e)","quote":"FCA 115(a): The family court has exclusive original jurisdiction over (i) abuse and neglect proceedings, as set forth in article ten; (ii) support proceedings, as set forth in article four; (iii) proceedings to determine paternity and for the support of children born out-of-wedlock, as set forth in article five; (iv) proceedings to permanently terminate parental rights to guardianship and custody of a child [...]; (v) proceedings concerning whether a person is in need of supervision, as set forth in article seven; and (vi) proceedings concerning juvenile delinquency as set forth in article three.\n\nFCA 115(b): The family court has such other jurisdiction as is set forth in this act, including jurisdiction over habeas corpus proceedings and over applications for support, maintenance, a distribution of marital property and custody in matrimonial actions when referred to the family court by the supreme court [...]\n\nFCA 115(e): The family court has concurrent jurisdiction with the criminal court over all family offenses as defined in article eight of this act.","from":"NYSenate.gov Open Legislation, official PDF FCT115, retrieved 2026-10-05 (docs/official-text/Family-Court-Act-exam-scope.md). May 31, 2026 exam-cutoff version and human review pending.","context":"Which cases only Family Court hears, which it shares, and what reaches it on referral.","url":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/FCT/115"},
          hook:"Six that are Family Court's alone: abuse and neglect, support, paternity, ending parental rights, PINS, delinquency. One it shares: family offenses (with criminal court). Divorce issues come in only when the supreme court sends them.",
          lore:["Specific matters, specific court.","Some doors only open here. One door is shared.","Divorce goes to supreme court."],
          related:["who_gets_a_lawyer","who_counts_as_family"],
